ss="av tinted" aria-hidden="true" style="--av-h: 150;">S</span><span class="sr-main"><span class="row-top"><span class="who">Skylark Notes</span><span class="addr"><span>mara@skylarknotes.example</span></span><span class="t num tog" title="Thu 23 Jul 2026, 13:48" data-stamp="">Thu 13:48</span></span><span class="row-mid"><span class="subj">#117 — the tools we keep</span></span><span class="prev" style="display: block;">On objects that age well.</span></span><span id=":rID:" hidden="" aria-label="Thu 23 Jul 2026, 13:48 · Read"></span></button><button type="button" class="row srow seen" data-id="f14" data-index="15" aria-label="Blattgang: Weekly wrap — the week in one read" aria-description="Thu 23 Jul 2026, 13:47 · Read" aria-describedby=":rID:" role="option" aria-setsize="3" aria-posinset="2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1"><span class="av tinted" aria-hidden="true" style="--av-h: 25;">B</span><span class="sr-main"><span class="row-top"><span class="who">Blattgang</span><span class="addr"><span>post@blattgang.press</span></span><span class="t num tog" title="Thu 23 Jul 2026, 13:47" data-stamp="">Thu 13:47</span></span><span class="row-mid"><span class="subj">Weekly wrap — the week in one read</span></span><span class="prev" style="display: block;">Everything lovely, compressed.</span></span><span id=":rID:" hidden="" aria-label="Thu 23 Jul 2026, 13:47 · Read"></span></button><button type="button" class="row srow seen" data-id="f15" data-index="16" aria-label="Wohnfalz: Sommer-Sale endet Sonntag" aria-description="Wed 22 Jul 2026, 13:46 · Read" aria-describedby=":rID:" role="option" aria-setsize="3" aria-posinset="3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1"><span class="av tinted" aria-hidden="true" style="--av-h: 250;">W</span><span class="sr-main"><span class="row-top"><span class="who">Wohnfalz</span><span class="addr"><span>news@wohnfalz.ch</span></span><span class="t num tog" title="Wed 22 Jul 2026, 13:46" data-stamp="">22 Jul</span></span><span class="row-mid"><span class="subj">Sommer-Sale endet Sonntag</span></span><span class="prev" style="display: block;">Letzte Chance auf Balkon-Lieblinge.</span></span><span id=":rID:" hidden="" aria-label="Wed 22 Jul 2026, 13:46 · Read"></span></button></div><div class="tail-row">An item turns read once it rests on screen in the stream. A quick scroll past leaves it unread.</div></div><div class="list-hints"><span class="key-hints"><span class="key-hint-move"><kbd>↓</kbd> Next in the stream</span><span class="key-hint-move"><kbd>←</kbd> Focus the menu</span><button type="button" class="key-hint" aria-haspopup="dialog"><kbd aria-hidden="true">?</kbd> shortcuts</button></span></div><div class="col-handle col-handle-list" role="separator" aria-orientation="vertical" aria-label="List width" aria-valuemin="320" aria-valuemax="320" aria-valuenow="400" tabindex="0"></div></div><div class="stream" aria-label="News stream"><div class="stream-top"><h1>News</h1><span class="meta num">12 new since you were here</span></div><div class="stream-hints"><span class="key-hints"><span class="key-hint-move"><kbd>↓</kbd> Next in the stream</span><span class="key-hint-move"><kbd>←</kbd> Focus the menu</span><button type="button" class="key-hint" aria-haspopup="dialog"><kbd aria-hidden="true">?</kbd> shortcuts</button></span></div><article class="scast cur" style="--sc-est: 200px;" data-sid="f1" data-unseen="1"><div class="sc-head"><div class="sc-line"><b>Skylark Notes</b><span class="addr"><span>mara@skylarknotes.example</span></span><span class="t num">09:02</span></div><h3>#118 — the joy of small tools</h3></div><div class="sc-clip"><p class="sc-body msg-plain">Hi there — this issue is a love letter to tools that do one thing kindly.

Small tools are not lesser tools. They are the ones that fit your hand on the first try: the ladle that pours without dripping, the pencil that starts every list, the app with exactly one screen. This week’s essay visits three workshops and asks each maker which object they’d save from a fire. Nobody picked the expensive one.

Worthy five: a lamp that ages like furniture, a wooden ladle from a two-person workshop, a pencil with honest graphite, a pocket notebook system, and one very good broom.

Also in this issue: an interview with a bookbinder on the pleasure of doing the same thing ten thousand times, and a reader thread about the tool you’ve owned longest.

The archive, as always, is open — issues #1 through #117, no paywall, no tracking.

— Mara</p><div class="sc-fade"></div></div><button type="button" class="sc-x" aria-expanded="false"><span>Expand</span><svg class="ic chev" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m6 3.8 4.2 4.2L6 12.2"></path></svg></button></article><article class="scast" style="--sc-est: 200px;" data-sid="f2" data-unseen="1"><div class="sc-head"><div class="sc-line"><b>Blattgang</b><span class="addr"><span>post@blattgang.press</span></span><span class="t num">08:31</span></div><h3>Why paper keeps winning</h3></div><div class="sc-clip"><p class="sc-body msg-plain">Three hundred years after the broadsheet, paper is still the best interface anyone has shipped: instant on, folds to pocket size, survives coffee, works in sunlight.

This week’s essay is about why the good digital tools all quietly imitate it — margins, pages, bookmarks, the satisfying flip — and what they still haven’t managed to copy. (Hint: it’s the smell, but it’s also the permission to scribble.)

From the mailbag: a dozen of you sent photos of your reading chairs. They are all magnificent. The armchair-with-lamp configuration leads by a wide margin.

Next week: a visit to a paper mill that has been run by the same family since 1874, and what their apprentice learned in year one. (Everything. She learned everything.)</p><div class="sc-fade"></div></div><button type="button" class="sc-x" aria-expanded="false"><span>Expand</span><svg class="ic chev" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m6 3.8 4.2 4.2L6 12.2"></path></svg></button></article><article class="scast" style="--sc-est: 200px;" data-sid="f3" data-unseen="1"><div class="sc-head"><div class="sc-line"><b>Wohnfalz</b><span class="addr"><span>news@wohnfalz.ch</span></span><span class="t num">07:44</span></div><h3>Ideen für kleine Räume</h3></div><div class="sc-clip"><p class="sc-body msg-plain">Kleine Räume, grosse Wirkung: Diese Woche zeigen wir Neuheiten, die sich zusammenklappen, stapeln oder ganz verschwinden, wenn der Tag sie nicht braucht.</p><figure class="sc-art" role="img" aria-label="Produktbild: Klapptisch, an der Wand montiert"><svg viewBox="0 0 520 216" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linecap="round"><rect x="0.7" y="0.7" width="518.6" height="214.6" rx="14" stroke="none" fill="var(--tint)"></rect><path d="M96 26v152"></path><path d="M60 178h404"></path><path d="M98 96h224"></path><path d="M98 104h224"></path><path d="M310 104l-46 74"></path><path d="M310 104l8 74"></path><circle cx="150" cy="86" r="9"></circle><path d="M159 86h7"></path><path d="M418 178v-64M404 114h28M410 100l8-14 8 14"></path></svg><figcaption>KLAPPRI — klappbar, wandmontiert</figcaption></figure><p class="sc-body msg-plain">Der Klapptisch KLAPPRI trägt vier Teller und einen Laptop — und hängt danach flach an der Wand. Dazu: ein Hocker, der Stauraum versteckt, und Haken, die keine Löcher hinterlassen.

Für Wohnfalz Mitglieder diese Woche: 15% auf alle Aufbewahrungsserien — im Showroom und online.</p><div class="sc-fade"></div></div><button type="button" class="sc-x" aria-expanded="false"><span>Expand</span><svg class="ic chev" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m6 3.8 4.2 4.2L6 12.2"></path></svg></button></article><article class="scast" style="--sc-est: 200px;" data-sid="f4" data-unseen="1"><div class="sc-head"><div class="sc-line"><b>The Maker’s Dozen</b><span class="addr"><span>hello@makersdozen.studio</span></span><span class="t num">07:12</span></div><h3>#41 — twelve things makers loved</h3></div><div class="sc-clip"><p class="sc-body msg-plain">Twelve things makers loved this month, and this one is a good batch.

The kiln timer that finally does ramps properly. A broom (yes, a second good broom this year — it’s a golden age). A price-your-work essay written by a potter who doubled her prices and lost exactly zero customers — required reading before your next market.

Also in the dozen: linen aprons that survive the wheel, a glaze-test tile system that ends the guessing, and a folding market table that one person can carry uphill.

The community thread this month: what did you make for yourself, not for sale? The answers are wonderful. A gate hinge. A soup bowl. A banjo.

Full list with photos and links below.</p><div class="sc-fade"></div></div><button type="button" class="sc-x" aria-expanded="false"><span>Expand</span><svg class="ic chev" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m6 3.8 4.2 4.2L6 12.2"></path></svg></button></article><article class="scast" style="--sc-est: 200px;" data-sid="f5" data-unseen="1"><div class="sc-head"><div class="sc-line"><b>Gratbrief</b><span class="addr"><span>post@gratbrief.ch</span></span><span class="t num">06:58</span></div><h3>This week’s hike: the Chäserrugg ridge</h3></div><div class="sc-clip"><p class="sc-body msg-plain">This week’s hike: the Chäserrugg ridge. Four hours, one ridge line, and the kind of views that make you forgive the first forty minutes of forest switchbacks.

Go early — the light on the Churfirsten before nine is the whole point. Coffee at the top station is honest; the rösti is better. Boots over trail runners: the ridge path has opinions.

Next week: a lake-to-lake traverse with a swim in the middle.</p><div class="sc-fade"></div></div><button type="button" class="sc-x" aria-expanded="false"><span>Expand</span><svg class="ic chev" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m6 3.8 4.2 4.2L6 12.2"></path></svg></button></article><article class="scast" style="--sc-est: 200px;" data-sid="f6" data-unseen="1"><div class="sc-head"><div class="sc-line"><b>Frühbrief Briefing</b><span class="addr"><span>briefing@fruehbrief.example</span></span><span class="t num">06:30</span></div><h3>Morgen-Briefing: Sommerfest-Wochenende</h3></div><div class="sc-clip"><p class="sc-body msg-plain">Guten Morgen. Das Sommerfest-Wochenende steht vor der Tür — in Winterthur werden über vierzig Quartierfeste erwartet, und die Wetterprognose spielt mit.

Ausserdem: Die Nachtzug-Teststrecke nach Barcelona ist auf den Herbst bestätigt, die Badis melden die wärmsten Wassertemperaturen seit fünf Jahren, und im Wallis beginnt die Aprikosenernte — süsser als letztes Jahr, sagen die Bauern.

Das Wetter: sonnig, am Nachmittag Quellwolken, 27 Grad. Perfektes Fest-Wetter.</p><div class="sc-fade"></div></div><button type="button" class="sc-x" aria-expanded="false"><span>Expand</span><svg class="ic chev" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m6 3.8 4.2 4.2L6 12.2"></path></svg></button></article><div aria-hidden="true" data-stream-tail="true" style="height: 1980px;"></div></div></section></div></main></div></div></div><div class="toast-slot" role="status" aria-live="polite"></div><div class="toast" role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>"
`;

